{# ── Chapter: Hook ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="hook">
  <h2>The card that looked perfect in the grid and broke in the sidebar</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      A shared <code>ProductCard</code> component ships in a three-column grid, where each card gets roughly 320px of horizontal space. It looks great, an image on the left, title and price on the right, all in one row. Someone reuses the same component in a "recently viewed" sidebar, and at the exact same browser window size, the card now has to fit into a strip that's only 220px wide. The image and text still try to sit side by side, because the CSS that decided "wide enough for a row layout" was never actually checking the card's own width, it was checking the browser window's width, which hadn't changed at all.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      The card's layout switch was written as <code>@media (min-width: 300px) { .card { flex-direction: row; } }</code>. That query is true or false for the whole page, based on the viewport, not per-instance. So the same component, rendered twice on the same page in two different-width containers, gets the exact same computed layout in both places, correct in the grid and wrong in the sidebar, and there's no version of a viewport media query that can tell those two placements apart.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      The root problem isn't the specific breakpoint value, it's that a media query's evaluation context is global to the document, there is exactly one viewport, so there is exactly one true/false answer per query, applied uniformly to every element that references it. A genuinely reusable component's ideal layout is a function of its own box's width, a per-instance, per-render-context value the viewport has no way to expose. Fixing this by adding more breakpoints, or JS-measuring the container and toggling a class, is treating a structural limitation as a tuning problem.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Chapter: Concept ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="concept">
  <h2>container-type: inline-size turns an element into a query target for its own descendants</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      Two pieces, both required. First, on the wrapping element (the grid cell, the sidebar slot), set <code>container-type: inline-size</code>, this tells the browser "let elements inside this box query its width." Second, inside the card's own CSS, replace the <code>@media</code> rule with <code>@container (min-width: 300px) { .card { flex-direction: row; } }</code>. Same-looking rule, completely different meaning: this one asks "is the <em>container</em> at least 300px," not "is the browser window at least 300px."
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      <code>container-type</code> establishes a <em>containment context</em>, the element it's set on becomes the thing <code>@container</code> queries measure against, found by walking up the DOM to the nearest ancestor with a container context (optionally narrowed with <code>container-name</code> if there's more than one candidate ancestor). Because that ancestor is a real, per-instance box in the render tree, the grid's 320px cell and the sidebar's 220px slot each get their own independent true/false answer to the same <code>@container</code> rule, using the same card CSS in both places.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      <code>inline-size</code> containment specifically constrains what the browser needs to compute before it can lay out the container's contents: it promises the container's own inline size doesn't depend on its children, which is what makes the query resolvable at all without a layout cycle that never terminates. That's also why you can't meaningfully query the same element that declares <code>container-type</code> on itself, the containment context has to be an ancestor. <code>container-name</code> exists for exactly the case where more than one ancestor in the chain declares a container context and an unscoped <code>@container</code> would resolve against the wrong one.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Chapter: Story ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="story-1">
  <h2>The same ProductCard, three placements, one CSS file</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      A design-system team's <code>ProductCard</code> shipped in a 3-column grid (~320px per card), a "recently viewed" sidebar (~220px), and a full-width featured-product hero slot (~900px+). The original media-query version needed a maintainer to know, ahead of time, every place the card would ever be dropped into, and hand-tune breakpoints per surface. Switching the card's own CSS to <code>@container</code> queries, and adding one line, <code>container-type: inline-size</code>, to each surface's wrapping element, made all three placements correct simultaneously, without the card's CSS caring which surface it was in.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      Before the fix, the sidebar instance was a special case: a <code>.sidebar .card</code> override forcing <code>flex-direction: column</code> regardless of viewport, a patch that worked only because someone remembered the sidebar was narrow and hand-wrote an exception for it. When the hero slot was added later, it needed its own override in the other direction. Two placements, two manual overrides, and a third placement would need a third. After the container-query rewrite, the override rules were deleted entirely, the card's own <code>@container</code> rule is correct in all three contexts because it's actually measuring each one.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      The interesting failure mode they'd been accumulating was that the "special case" overrides were per-surface, not per-card, so every new surface the card got dropped into needed a new override written by whoever added it, a maintenance cost that scaled with the number of places the component was reused, which is exactly backwards for a component meant to be reused freely. Container queries move that cost to zero per new surface: the wrapping element needs one <code>container-type</code> declaration (often already true of any real layout container), and the card's own CSS handles the rest.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

