{# ── Chapter: FE Depth ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="fe-depth">
  <h2>Containment has a cost, and container-type isn't free-form</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      <code>container-type: inline-size</code> only lets you query the container's width, not its height, that covers the vast majority of real component reflows (most cards care about "how wide am I," not "how tall is my parent"). If a component genuinely needs to query both axes, <code>container-type: size</code> does that, but it requires the container to have an explicit height, since querying block-size containment on an auto-height box would create a layout cycle the browser can't resolve.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      Containment changes what the browser is allowed to assume, which is why it isn't automatically applied everywhere: an element with <code>inline-size</code> containment can't let its own width be influenced by its content's natural width (no more shrink-to-fit based on children in that axis), that's the trade the browser needs in exchange for making the box a stable, queryable size. Most layout containers (grid cells, flex items with a defined width, sidebar columns) already behave this way in practice, so the constraint is rarely felt; it matters when someone reaches for <code>container-type</code> on an element whose width was supposed to be content-driven.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      Container query units (<code>cqw</code>, <code>cqi</code>, <code>cqb</code>, <code>cqmin</code>, <code>cqmax</code>) are worth knowing alongside the at-rule itself, <code>font-size: clamp(0.85rem, 4cqi, 1.1rem)</code> lets type scale with the container continuously instead of stepping at breakpoints, which is the more common real need for deeply nested reusable components than a hard flex-direction flip. And because <code>@container</code> resolution happens per containment-context ancestor, a deeply nested component tree with containers at every level can mean more layout recalculation on resize than a single document-level media query, for most component-library use (a handful of container ancestors, not hundreds), this is not a practical concern, but it's the actual mechanism behind "don't declare container-type on every div out of habit."
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

