{# ── Chapter: Takeaway ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="takeaway">
  <h2>Remember</h2>
  <div class="case-takeaway">
    <h3>Key takeaways</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <div class="tone-junior">A media query only ever answers "how wide is the browser window," so two differently-sized placements of the same component can get the same layout by mistake. container-type: inline-size + @container answers "how wide is this specific box," so each placement gets the right one.</div>
        <div class="tone-mid">Put container-type: inline-size on the wrapping element (the grid cell, the sidebar slot), then swap the component's own @media rules for @container rules — same syntax shape, but now scoped to that ancestor's actual rendered width instead of the viewport.</div>
        <div class="tone-staff">Containment has real constraints (the container can't size itself off its own contents in the queried axis), and @container units (cqw/cqi/etc.) are often a better fit than a hard breakpoint for deeply reusable components — reach for those before reaching for more media-query overrides per surface.</div>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>
